\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Coefficients of the \qty {10}{\mm } Festo BPA pressure model of Equation~(\ref {eq:BPAFit}), corrected from \citet {hunt_modeling_2017}.}}{19}{table.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Maximum force equation coefficient values with confidence intervals (CI) and goodness-of-fit measures. Equation~(\ref {eq:Fmax_3D}) is compared to data taken at \qty {620}{\kPa }. Equations~(\ref {eq:Fmax10}) and (\ref {eq:Fmax20}) are compared against maximum force from using the Festo tool. It was not necessary to fit an adjusted $\text {R}^2$ in \mbox {these cases.}}}{56}{table.caption.23}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Normalized isometric BPA force equation (Equation~(\ref {eq:Fstar})) coefficient values and goodness-of-fit measures.}}{56}{table.caption.23}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Goodness-of-fit comparison for the Bolen, Sárosi, and Martens and Boblan models at different resting lengths (in mm) for \qty {10}{\mm } and \qty {20}{\mm } BPAs. RMSE and maximum error are reported in newtons, and FVU denotes the fraction of variance unexplained.}}{65}{table.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Pre-optimization pinned-knee flexor torque-prediction goodness of fit per test, comparing the three pre-optimization torque predictions of Figure~\ref {fig:FlxPin_preopt}: the rigid-body prediction, the unoptimized prediction of the improved BPA characterization, and the back-calculated (hybrid) torque. The rigid-body prediction is undefined near full extension, where its modeled strain falls below zero, so the rigid-body and improved-characterization columns are scored head-to-head on the measured points of each test at which the rigid-body prediction is defined (75 of 90, 17 of 20, 14 of 19, and 15 of 22 points, in panel order); the back-calculated torque is scored on all measured points. RMSE and maximum absolute residual (Max.) are in $\text {N}\cdot \text {m}$, and FVU denotes the fraction of variance unexplained. Training and validation follow the adopted cross-validation fold.}}{102}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Pinned-knee flexor torque-prediction goodness of fit per test. The zero-correction prediction uses the improved BPA force characterization alone (rigid attachments, no length offset, and no wrapping loss); the corrected model adds the adopted correction terms of the two-stage identification (Section~\ref {sec:identification}). RMSE and maximum absolute residual (Max.) are in $\text {N}\cdot \text {m}$. In the adopted cross-validation fold the $l_{0}=\qty {48.5}{\cm }$ and \qty {41.0}{\cm } tests formed the training set and the $l_{0}=\qty {45.7}{\cm }$ and \qty {40.6}{\cm } tendon tests were held out for validation; the $l_{0}=\qty {47.9}{\cm }$ test was not used in the identification and is reported in Appendix~\ref {app:model_geometry}.}}{103}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Pinned-knee extensor torque-prediction goodness of fit per test, at the adopted correction terms (stiffness pair locked to the flexor-identified values). The zero-correction prediction uses the improved BPA force characterization alone; the corrected model adds the extensor-specific terms $(\chi _{0}, \chi _{3})$. RMSE and maximum absolute residual (Max.) are in $\text {N}\cdot \text {m}$. The extensor terms were trained on the $l_{0}=\qty {43.6}{\cm }$, \qty {43.2}{\cm } tendon, and \qty {45.7}{\cm } tests and validated on the $l_{0}=\qty {40.0}{\cm }$, \qty {40.0}{\cm } tendon, and \qty {46.5}{\cm } tests; the three remaining extensor tests were not used (Appendix~\ref {app:model_geometry}).}}{107}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Goodness of fit for the improved torque model across test configurations. RMSE and maximum absolute residual are in $\text {N}\cdot \text {m}$.}}{112}{table.caption.42}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Adopted correction terms of the two-stage identification. The extensor refit locked the effective-stiffness pair $(\chi _{1}, \chi _{2})$ to the flexor-identified values, so a single stiffness pair is shared across the flexor and extensor configurations, and only $\chi _{0}$ and $\chi _{3}$ were re-identified for the extensor. The pair was adopted from a family of near-equivalent fits by a requirement external to the identification, namely that both route-redesign configurations meet the required \qty {5}{\percent } torque-overshoot target simultaneously. The stiffness terms are effective system stiffnesses that lump the compliance of the brackets, fixtures, and winch cable; $\chi _{3}$ is unitless and scales the fictive-strain wrapping loss.}}{114}{table.caption.43}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Bi-pulley batch on five muscle sites of the robot-body model's OpenSim leg. Objective 101 is the batch's infeasibility sentinel; all five runs closed with a nonpositive torque margin. The soleus and tibialis-anterior runs converged feasibly (pattern-search exit flag 1), and their objectives are the normalized torque shortfalls; the remaining three runs exited with flag $-2$.}}{121}{table.caption.46}%
